# Dagric OS — Base system

# Live boot machinery (required for the live ISO to boot)
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
user-setup

# Kernel
linux-image-amd64
# The initramfs compressor. /etc/initramfs-tools/initramfs.conf already says
# COMPRESS=zstd — that is Debian's own Trixie default — but /usr/bin/zstd was
# not in the image, so mkinitramfs printed one warning into a fifteen-minute
# build log and silently fell back to gzip. Two correct decisions cancelling
# each other: initramfs-tools-core carries "Recommends: zstd" and auto/config
# sets --apt-recommends false, so the recommendation was dropped and nothing
# ever said so. The shipped 1.0 initrd.img is 161 MB of gzip; the same content
# as zstd is 140 MB, and generating it on the two cores an owner's laptop
# actually has takes 2 s instead of 7 s. GRUB reads that file at roughly
# 13 MB/s off a USB stick, so it is also the length of the one boot screen with
# no logo on it. zstd is strictly better than gzip on both axes here.
zstd

# Networking
network-manager
wireless-regdb

# WI-FI DOES NOT WORK WITHOUT THIS, AND EVERY ISO SHIPPED SO FAR WAS MISSING IT.
#
# NetworkManager reaches wireless hardware only through a supplicant. It
# Recommends wpasupplicant rather than depending on it, auto/config passes
# --apt-recommends false, and nothing pinned it — so it was dropped from every
# build. Confirmed in four committed build logs: "wpasupplicant" appears exactly
# once in each, inside apt's "Recommended packages:" block, and the words
# "Unpacking wpasupplicant" and "Setting up wpasupplicant" appear nowhere. There
# is no iwd either, so there was no second backend to fall back to.
#
# The result is worse than a missing driver, because it looks like nothing at
# all: firmware-iwlwifi, -brcm80211, -atheros and -realtek are all present, so
# the adapter is detected, and then the network applet simply lists no networks.
# Open or WPA, live USB or installed, both editions.
#
# Sold to people leaving Windows 10 on a LAPTOP, this is the first thing they
# test and the fastest possible refund. It survived to 1.0 because the two
# environments it was tested in — QEMU, which has no wireless to emulate, and a
# wired desktop — are precisely the two that cannot see it.
#
# wireless-regdb one line up is the tell: it is another NetworkManager Recommends
# from the very same apt block, it WAS pinned, and it is installed in the same
# logs. The pin is the whole difference.
wpasupplicant
# "SHARE THIS MACHINE'S CONNECTION" NEEDS THIS, AND IT WAS ON THE SAME DROPPED
# LINE AS wpasupplicant ABOVE.
#
# app-network.html lists "Share this machine's connection" among the five things
# an owner will do — set the Wi-Fi mode to Access Point to tether a tablet.
# NetworkManager serves DHCP and DNS to the clients of a shared/AP connection
# only by spawning the dnsmasq binary from dnsmasq-base; NM has no built-in DHCP
# server for that mode. Without the binary the radio comes up in AP mode and no
# client ever gets an address — a documented feature that silently does nothing.
#
# It is a NetworkManager Recommends, so --apt-recommends false drops it, and it
# sat literally next to wpasupplicant in apt's "Recommended packages:" block —
# missed in the same sweep that pinned wpasupplicant. It was present on Pro only
# by accident, pulled in by libvirt (pro-vm.list.chroot). dnsmasq-base is the
# on-demand BINARY, not the system dnsmasq DAEMON, so pinning it adds no
# listening service at boot — NM invokes it only when a share is started.
dnsmasq-base
# VPN import — a privacy-first OS that can't open a WireGuard/OpenVPN config
# undercuts its own pitch. WireGuard is kernel-native; these add the applet
# handlers. (Found by test review: import silently failed.)
network-manager-openvpn
network-manager-openconnect
# L2TP/IPsec covers the corporate VPN stack many institutions still run.
network-manager-l2tp

# Audio — PipeWire only, no PulseAudio daemon
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-audio
wireplumber

# THE WI-FI BUG AGAIN, IN AUDIO. Same shape, same cause, same silence.
#
# alsa-ucm-conf carries the Use Case Manager profiles that tell ALSA how to
# route a modern laptop's codec — which outputs exist, which amplifiers to
# enable, how the speakers and headphone jack are actually wired. On anything
# using Intel SOF or AMD ACP, which is most laptops built since roughly 2019,
# missing UCM means no sound at all, or sound from one channel, or headphones
# that never engage.
#
# The tell is that firmware-sof-signed IS already pinned in
# firmware.list.chroot. So the image ships the FIRMWARE for these codecs and
# not the configuration that drives them — the machine has everything it needs
# to make noise and no instructions for doing it.
#
# It is a Recommends of the ALSA stack, --apt-recommends false drops it, and
# nothing pinned it. Confirmed the same way as wpasupplicant: the string occurs
# exactly once in each committed build log, at out/free-rebuild.log:2430, inside
# the "Recommended packages:" block that spans 2422-2463, and "Setting up
# alsa-ucm-conf" appears nowhere in any log.
alsa-ucm-conf

# And rtkit, dropped from the very same block (out/free-rebuild.log:2448).
#
# RealtimeKit is what lets PipeWire ask for realtime scheduling priority.
# Without it the audio thread competes with everything else, so sound crackles
# and drops out under load — compiling, a browser with thirty tabs, a game.
# It is the difference between "the audio works" and "the audio is reliable",
# and it is the kind of fault an owner blames on the whole operating system
# rather than on a missing 40 kB package.
rtkit

# Printing — cups alone doesn't cover most hardware. Gutenprint drives the
# majority of inkjets/lasers, ipp-usb gives driverless modern printers,
# cups-filters is the print pipeline. (Found by test review: printing was
# under-provisioned.) HP all-in-ones still want hplip via the welcome helper.
cups
cups-filters
printer-driver-gutenprint
ipp-usb
# ipp-usb one line up only covers printers that speak IPP-over-USB, which in
# practice means printers made from roughly 2017 onwards — not the hardware
# generation this OS is sold to rescue. THIS is the piece that makes an ORDINARY
# USB printer appear when it is plugged in: it ships the udev rule and
# udev-configure-printer, which creates the CUPS queue on hotplug.
#
# Without it, dagric-hardware-check tells the owner in plain English "Dagric will
# set it up for you after installing", app-printing.html says the drivers were
# "already installed before you plugged anything in", and then nothing happens at
# all — no queue, no notification, no printer in any print dialogue. The DRIVER
# was on the image (printer-driver-gutenprint above); the thing that turns a
# plugged-in printer into a queue was not.
#
# Another Recommends dropped by --apt-recommends false, same shape as
# wpasupplicant and alsa-ucm-conf further up: it sits in apt's own "Recommended
# packages:" block at out/free-rebuild.log:2456, and "Setting up
# system-config-printer-udev" appears in no build log. python3-cups and
# python3-cupshelpers are hard Depends, so they arrive with it regardless of the
# recommends setting; python3 is already pinned above.
system-config-printer-udev
# "Print to PDF" as a virtual printer in every app's print dialog — a
# baseline Windows-switcher expectation (audit gap: was absent).
#
# THE PACKAGE ALONE DOES NOT GIVE YOU THE QUEUE, and for four builds it did not.
# Its postinst creates the "PDF" printer itself and cannot do that in a build
# chroot — policy-rc.d denies the cups reload, so it prints "Skipped automated
# creation of the PDF queue." and exits 0 — and dpkg then records the package
# configured forever, so it never retries on the installed machine either. The
# queue is created on first boot instead, by
# config/includes.chroot/etc/systemd/system/dagric-pdf-queue.service, enabled
# and path-checked by config/hooks/normal/0810-print-to-pdf.hook.chroot. Remove
# either of those and this line puts the driver back on the image with no
# printer called PDF in any print dialogue — which is what shipped until now,
# against three written promises (site/index.html, site/features.html and the
# manual's app-printing.html).
printer-driver-cups-pdf

# Scanners — a print-only distro that can't scan reads as broken to anyone
# with an all-in-one. SANE backend + a simple KDE scan app.
sane-utils
skanlite
# Driverless NETWORK print/scan (AirPrint/Mopria/eSCL): avahi discovers the
# devices, sane-airscan talks to network scanners without vendor drivers.
avahi-daemon
sane-airscan
# avahi DISCOVERS the printer; this is what RESOLVES it. libnss-mdns is the NSS
# module that teaches getaddrinfo() about .local names, and it is an avahi-daemon
# Recommends dropped by --apt-recommends false — it sits in apt's "Recommended
# packages:" block at out/free-rebuild.log:2423 and "Setting up libnss-mdns"
# appears in no build log. Same omission as wpasupplicant and alsa-ucm-conf above.
#
# Leaving it out gives this failure its most misleading possible shape, because
# avahi still browses: the printer IS discovered, DOES appear in Add Printer and
# in the print dialogue by name, and then cannot be reached. CUPS' temporary
# queue for a DNS-SD printer — the "usually appears without you doing anything"
# path app-printing.html advertises — carries an ipp://NAME.local URI, and the
# ipp backend resolves that through getaddrinfo(), not through Avahi. So the job
# fails on a printer the owner just watched appear. Same for typing
# http://printer.local to reach its web page, and for reaching any other machine
# on the LAN by name.
#
# The package's own postinst is what adds the mdns entry to /etc/nsswitch.conf;
# nothing in this tree ships or rewrites that file, so the edit stands.
libnss-mdns

# Firmware updates (LVFS) — Discover already surfaces these; the backend
# was missing. Matters for a distro sold to run on real hardware.
fwupd

# Locales — without this, every terminal shows locale warnings
# (found by the install run-through test)
locales
# gettext-base ships /usr/bin/gettext and /usr/bin/gettext.sh, which every
# dagric-* tool now sources through /usr/bin/dagric-i18n.sh. It is Priority:
# standard and does arrive implicitly today (verified in out/build-free.log),
# but arriving implicitly is exactly how Orca lost its speech engine and how
# python3 nearly lost the migration assistant: --apt-recommends false plus a
# changed Priority upstream is all it takes. The failure mode is silent — every
# tool keeps running and every one of them speaks English on a German machine —
# so pin it. 0150-locales.hook.chroot warns at build time if it is absent, but
# a warning is not a gate.
gettext-base

# Windows-switcher care: read the drives people bring with them
ntfs-3g
exfatprogs

# Migration Assistant (dagric-migrate) — rsync does the resumable copying
# from the old Windows partition. Free: migration IS the onboarding.
rsync
# python3 powers the bookmark/tab/password recovery in migrate-browser.py.
# It arrives today only as another package's dependency, and with
# --apt-recommends false that is exactly how Orca lost its speech engine.
# Pin it: a core free-edition feature must not rest on an implicit dep.
python3

# python3-xlib used to sit here, for a helper that rewrote the first-run
# wizard's _NET_WM_ICON from outside the process to get the Dagric logo onto its
# title bar. It is gone, and so is the helper: Qt tells the compositor which
# desktop file it belongs to on BOTH display servers — app_id on Wayland,
# _KDE_NET_WM_DESKTOP_FILE on X11, both reading "org.qt-project.qml" — and
# writing that one desktop file makes KWin draw the right icon before the window
# is ever mapped. See config/hooks/normal/0540-qml-identity.hook.chroot.

# Drive health — smartctl feeds the Security Checkup's disk report, and
# plasma-disks (desktop list) warns the owner BEFORE a failing disk dies.
# "You own this machine" includes knowing when its disk is dying.
smartmontools

# Archive formats Ark should just handle
p7zip-full
unzip

# Small CLI plumbing the helpers rely on: xdg-open (open URLs/files in the
# user's default app) is used by dagric-get-resolve; unzip is used by the
# Resolve guide. With --apt-recommends false these aren't pulled implicitly.
xdg-utils

# This one is a pin, not a nice-to-have. xdg-user-dirs writes
# ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs at first login, and that file is the ONLY thing that
# creates Documents, Downloads, Pictures, Music and Videos — with the owner's own
# language for the names. It is a Recommends of half the desktop, and
# --apt-recommends false drops it, so today it lands on Pro (dragged in as some
# Pro app's hard dependency) and NOT on free. Verified in out/free-rebuild.log,
# where it sits in the "Recommended packages:" block and is never set up.
#
# A Windows switcher whose home folder has no Documents is the worst possible
# first five minutes, and it breaks "free is leaner, never broken". Two shipped
# code paths already read this file and quietly degrade without it: the
# 2010-dagric-hwcheck-icon live hook falls back to an English "Desktop" for a
# German owner, and dagric-hardware-check saves its report to $HOME instead.
xdg-user-dirs

# Laptop power profiles (performance / balanced / power-save in the tray)
power-profiles-daemon

# Security & updates
sudo
firewalld
plasma-firewall
unattended-upgrades
ca-certificates
# AppArmor — mandatory access control. Confines apps to what they legitimately
# need, so a compromised browser/PDF viewer can't roam the whole system.
# The -extra profiles cover far more of the desktop than the base set.
apparmor
apparmor-profiles
apparmor-profiles-extra
apparmor-utils
# Lynis — on-demand security audit with a hardening score. A "Security
# Checkup" a non-expert can run and understand. (Pro adds proactive
# monitoring; this baseline audit is for everyone.)
lynis

# Kup — KDE-native, no-config scheduled backup in System Settings, for
# non-technical users. (Pro keeps Vorta/Borg for encrypted/deduplicated repos.)
kup-backup

# App distribution
flatpak

# Fonts — small, complete, readable
fonts-noto-core
fonts-noto-color-emoji
fonts-liberation
# Metric-compatible with Calibri/Cambria so .docx/.xlsx from MS Office open
# in LibreOffice without layout reflow. Tiny; huge fidelity win.
fonts-crosextra-carlito
fonts-crosextra-caladea
# CJK fallback (~2 MB). Without ANY CJK-capable font, a Japanese product page,
# a Chinese manual, or a Korean video title renders as empty tofu boxes — which
# reads as "this OS is broken", not "this OS is lean". Droid Fallback is the
# minimum that makes text legible everywhere; full-quality CJK typography
# (fonts-noto-cjk, ~91 MB) stays the Pro upgrade.
fonts-droid-fallback

# Multimedia codecs — mpv bundles ffmpeg for local files, but Plasma/Qt and
# GStreamer apps need these for web/embedded media to play with sound.
libavcodec-extra
gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad
gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly
gstreamer1.0-libav
gstreamer1.0-vaapi
# GPU-accelerated video decode (VA-API) — 4K playback without pegging the CPU
va-driver-all

# Double-clicking a downloaded .deb (Chrome, Zoom, VS Code) should just work
gdebi

# Compressed swap — snappier on low-RAM machines
zram-tools

# The other half of the low-RAM story. With zram on, the kernel's own OOM
# killer becomes very reluctant to fire: it can always compress one more page,
# so a 4 GB machine that runs out spends minutes thrashing instead of seconds
# failing. To the owner that is not "out of memory", it is "the computer froze
# and I had to hold the power button" — the exact experience they left Windows
# to escape. earlyoom turns it into "Firefox closed". The 0250-performance hook
# configures it (with an --avoid list that keeps the Plasma session itself
# alive) and enables the service.
earlyoom

# update-desktop-database, for the plain-English app-name overrides in
# /usr/local/share/applications (see /usr/bin/dagric-app-names). KDE reads
# desktop files through KSycoca and does not need it, but the GTK apps Pro
# ships resolve "Open With" through GIO, which reads mimeinfo.cache. ~50 kB,
# and nothing else on the image pulls it in.
desktop-file-utils

# Snapshot recovery (free): snapper takes automatic pre-update snapshots of the
# btrfs root; grub-btrfs (vendored in 0800 hook) exposes them in the boot menu
# so a broken update can be rolled back from GRUB. inotify-tools drives the
# grub-btrfs daemon; gawk is its generator dependency. (Pro keeps Timeshift's
# scheduled GUI as the step-up.) kdialog/notify power the setup helpers.
snapper
inotify-tools
gawk
kdialog
libnotify-bin

# THE RESTORE HALF. snapper takes the snapshots and, until now, nothing on the
# free edition could put one back without a terminal — the GRUB submenu boots a
# snapshot read-only, which is a look, not a recovery. btrfs-assistant is the
# missing GUI: pick a snapshot, press restore, reboot.
#
# It is Qt6, so it belongs on a Plasma 6 desktop rather than sitting there as an
# obvious GTK bolt-on, and it reads snapper's own configs rather than inventing
# a parallel scheme. Its restore does the subvolume swap that works on the plain
# @/@home layout Calamares installs.
#
# This matters most for the machines that have nobody watching them. A school or
# library PC that will not boot after an update is a ticket and a visit; a
# librarian who can pick "yesterday" from a list and press a button is neither.
btrfs-assistant

# Scheduled scrub, so silent corruption is found while the snapshots still hold
# a good copy of the extent. Nothing on a stock Debian install ever runs
# `btrfs scrub`, and the target hardware for this product is a decade-old disk
# that has been spinning since Windows 7. It also gives btrfs-assistant its
# Maintenance tab, so the two land together deliberately.
btrfsmaintenance

# Branded boot splash (theme in /usr/share/plymouth/themes/dagric)
plymouth
plymouth-themes

# Laptop hardware: fingerprint login (standard on business laptops) and
# Intel thermal management (quieter, cooler under load).
fprintd
libpam-fprintd
thermald

# HYBRID GRAPHICS — the standard build of every 2013-2018 gaming and creator
# laptop, which is a large share of the machines this OS is sold to rescue.
#
# Those laptops have an Intel iGPU wired to the screen and an NVIDIA or AMD dGPU
# for work that needs it. Without a D-Bus service to arbitrate, everything runs
# on the integrated chip and the discrete card sits idle and unreachable: no
# right-click "run this on the dedicated card", no way to send Blender or a game
# to the hardware bought for it. dagric-hardware-check already DETECTS this
# configuration and reports it, which made the absence of any way to act on it
# the more annoying half of the problem.
#
# switcheroo-control is 16 kB in main and is a plain systemd D-Bus service; the
# desktop hooks it automatically, so this needs no configuration. It defaults to
# the integrated GPU to save battery — the right default for a laptop — and the
# discrete card is then one menu item away.
switcheroo-control

# Quality of life
bash-completion
less
nano
# vim as well as nano, on BOTH editions, and it is not a duplicate.
#
# nano is the right default for the audience: a switcher who opens a config file
# needs the keys printed at the bottom of the screen. But `vim` is muscle memory
# for anyone who has ever administered a Linux box, and it is also what every
# tutorial, forum answer and README on the internet tells you to type. A
# terminal that answers `vim: command not found` reads as a broken or crippled
# system to exactly the confident user most likely to recommend Dagric to
# somebody else — and Debian's default `vi` (from vim-tiny, pulled in as a
# dependency) is worse than absent, because it opens and then behaves like
# neither vim nor anything else the person expects.
#
# vim-nox and the full vim-gtk3 are both wrong here: nox drags in a scripting
# stack for a text editor, gtk3 adds a GUI nobody asked for. Plain `vim` is the
# terminal build.
vim
curl
plocate
# lspci — hardware detection (the dagric-drivers helper depends on it;
# found by code review: it was never installed)
pciutils
